          "text": "THIS TELOS DECLARES NO WITNESSES, DELIBERATELY. Not an oversight, and not work waiting to be done -- recorded here so a later session reads the reason instead of re-deriving it or, worse, closing the gap with a witness that cannot fail.\n\nIn its plain reading this is a MAINTENANCE telos: raw data retained and never collapsed is a property that must keep holding, and the day after the first cache lands is when it is under load. day cannot express that today. `assess telos` is cumulative by design and a claim probe ignores --scope by design, so any witness available now would report met forever after the first pass -- day#138's failure, and what day#86 calls worse than leaving a telos unwitnessed. telos/cross-harness-maintained hit the same wall and was deliberately left undeclared; it is filed as day#171, a design question about the telos model rather than a missing probe.\n\nWhat the witness interview produced instead is telos/raw-data-and-projections-attained, which asks whether the machinery that makes this principle CHECKABLE has been built: a specification of the read/write surface, every stored value declared raw or derived, and a conformance test binding that declaration to the code under the migration matrix's own rule -- a stored value with no committed row is an error, not a pass. That half is attainment, reports met forever once reached, and is witnessed by `spec-conformance`, which reports absent today because the test does not exist yet.\n\nSo the division of labour is: day witnesses the attainment, CI enforces the maintenance. `day assess telos raw-data-and-projections` continuing to say this telos declares no witnesses is the honest reading, not a gap to close.",
